Drive an objective to completion in the current directory.

Usage:
  flynn goal [flags] "<objective>"
  flynn goal --goal-spec <file> [flags] ["<objective>"]

Works the objective in the current directory until it reports done, stops at a
limit, or is stopped. The run is recorded: 'flynn inspect <run-id>' replays it
and 'flynn spine verify <run-id>' checks the record.

Run flags go before goal or between goal and the objective. Put -- before an
objective that starts with a dash.

Approval and irreversible actions:

--require-approval <action> pauses that action until a person authorizes it. The
interactive session asks; a run with nobody to ask refuses the action. Every
decision is recorded on the run's stream, and a name the run does not offer is
refused.

--irreversible <action> marks an action as reaching outside the workspace and
not undoable. It adds a check on top of the run's normal grant and sandbox and
takes none away: an action the grant refuses stays refused, and one it permits
is also held until --allow declares it. A run that reaches an undeclared one
stops and asks.

--allow <action> declares in advance that the run may take an action marked
--irreversible. It only lifts that hold, so it needs a matching --irreversible,
and it grants nothing the run's normal grant and sandbox do not already permit.
It is how a person who is not present authorizes an irreversible action in
writing; one is never inferred from the objective.

Goal spec files:

A goal spec file states the terms of a run: what must stay true while it works.
Each term is audited after every step, before the goal is asked whether it is
done, and a breach stops the run naming the term. Pass it with --goal-spec ("-"
reads stdin):

  {
    "objective": "upgrade the http client and keep the suite green",
    "stopCondition": "the client is upgraded and the suite passes",
    "invariants": [
      {"id": "public-api",
       "statement": "the exported API of ./client does not change",
       "check": "./dev/apidiff ./client"}
    ],
    "allowances": [{"action": "shell", "target": "prod"}]
  }

Every field is optional as long as the file states an objective or one term; a
file of nothing but terms takes its objective from the command line. A term
whose "check" is omitted is ruled on by the auditor model reading the run's
record, which is weaker than running a command, so write the check where you
can. A term claiming something is not there needs one, since the record cannot
show an absence.

A check runs as semi-trusted work and needs a host that can contain it, as
--require-proof does; where it cannot be run, the run stops saying so instead of
carrying on unaudited.

Flags:
  --verify <command>           a command run after the agent stops that checks
                               the goal succeeded
  --require-proof              refuse success over a plan item the record shows
                               no passing check for
  --goal-spec <file>           read the run's terms from a JSON file ("-" is
                               stdin)
  --require-approval <action>  pause this action until a person authorizes it
                               (repeatable)
  --irreversible <action>      mark this action as not undoable, held until
                               --allow (repeatable)
  --allow <action>             permit this --irreversible action in advance
                               (repeatable)
  --fanout                     let the goal delegate sub-tasks to concurrent
                               child agents
  --max-cost <amount>          cap the run's model and tool spend at this
                               amount; 0 is unlimited
  --max-tokens <n>             cap the run's metered tokens at n; 0 is unlimited

Examples:
  flynn goal "audit the repo for TODOs and write a summary to NOTES.md"
  flynn goal --verify "go test ./..." "fix the failing parser test"
  flynn goal --goal-spec terms.json --max-cost 5
  flynn goal --require-approval bash -- "-v is ignored; find out why"

Run 'flynn help flags' for the flags every command accepts.
